{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Getting started · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Getting started with php-via</h1>
    <p>
        php-via is a PHP framework for server-side reactive UIs. Your app runs as a long-lived
        <a href="https://openswoole.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSwoole</a> server, and
        <a href="https://data-star.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Datastar</a> applies the server's
        updates in the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
        You write signals, actions and views in PHP. Each tab receives new HTML and signal values over one
        SSE stream, so you write no JavaScript, run no build step and keep no separate API.
    </p>

    <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>PHP 8.4+</li>
        <li><a href="https://openswoole.com/docs/get-started/installation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSwoole PHP extension</a> 26+</li>
        <li>Composer</li>
        <li><a href="https://pecl.php.net/package/brotli" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brotli PHP extension</a> <em>(optional: static files get Brotli level 11 once it is loaded, pages and SSE with <code>Config::withBrotli()</code>; see <a href="/docs/deployment#static-compression">Static compression</a>)</em></li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="installation">Installation</h2>
    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
composer require mbolli/php-via
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="your-first-app">Your first app</h2>
    <p>
        Create <code>app.php</code> in a new directory and put this counter in it. It is a complete
        php-via app:
    </p>

    <div class="docs-demo">
        <div class="docs-demo-header">The counter, running on this server</div>
        <div class="docs-demo-body">
            {{ demo | raw }}
        </div>
    </div>

    {% code 'bash' %}{% verbatim %}
php app.php
# [INFO] Via server started on http://0.0.0.0:3000
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>Open <a href="http://localhost:3000" target="_blank">localhost:3000</a> in your browser and click the button. A second tab starts again at 0, because each tab keeps its own count. That is <strong>TAB scope</strong>, the default.</p>

    <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
    <p>A php-via page goes through these steps:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>php-via runs your page handler and sends the initial HTML.</li>
        <li>The signal values come with it as JSON, in a <code>&lt;meta data-signals&gt;</code> tag.</li>
        <li>Datastar opens an SSE connection to <code>/_sse</code>.</li>
        <li>A click makes Datastar POST to <code>/_action/{id}</code>.</li>
        <li>Your action runs and changes signals, and php-via renders the view again.</li>
        <li>The patch arrives over SSE, and Datastar morphs it into the page.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The page does not reload. Datastar changes only the elements that differ.</p>

    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/signals">Signals</a>: reactive state and two-way binding</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/scopes">Scopes</a>: share state across users with one line</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/components">Components</a>: compose complex pages from isolated sub-contexts</li>
        <li><a href="/examples">Examples</a>: running examples with their PHP and Twig source</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="professional-support">Professional support</h2>
    <p>
        Need help with architecture, migration, or scaling a server-centric PHP application?
        <a href="https://zweiundeins.gmbh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">zwei und eins gmbh</a>
        (the company behind php-via) offers audits and implementation support.
    </p>
</article>

{% endblock %}
